+/* ======================================================================
+   THE ROUND, WATCHED
+
+   Seven seconds, three beats, and one fact a results table cannot show:
+   DEMAND and SALES are different numbers. The gap is the people who chose
+   you and could not buy, and the engine hands them to whoever still had
+   stock. That is the whole picture. Everything else stays in the table
+   underneath, which is where it reads better anyway.
+
+   WHAT IT IS ALLOWED TO DRAW. A pile is a company's share of demand, which
+   is public — it is in the market table after every round — and it is also
+   already arithmetic: a seat knows its own demand and its own share, so it
+   knows the size of the whole market, so it knows what everybody else's
+   share is worth in buyers. The drawing adds nothing to what the page had
+   already handed over.
+
+   What is NOT public is how much a rival could actually supply. So a rival
+   who ran out gets the words and no number — the page already says they
+   ran out — while your own stockout carries its exact figure, because those
+   are your books. A better-looking picture is not a reason to widen what a
+   seat can see.
+
+   The clock is the only state. Every position and every number is a pure
+   function of FILM.t, which is what makes Skip one line and resize free.
+   ====================================================================== */
